staff relations policy were approved by council in response

01:57:31.010 --> 01:57:35.030
to an amendment to the Municipal act that required municipalities to

01:57:35.530 --> 01:57:38.270
have both documents and appoint an Integrity Commissioner.

01:57:39.230 --> 01:57:43.350
Throughout this term. We've had John Mascaran, Council's Integrity Commissioner,

01:57:43.850 --> 01:57:47.950
come in. He presented during the orientation and just this past October

01:57:48.590 --> 01:57:51.790
about both documents as well as the case

01:57:52.290 --> 01:57:55.430
law that's arisen since 2018 with

01:57:55.930 --> 01:57:59.670
respect to, to them. So the code also directs

01:58:00.170 --> 01:58:03.150
staff to review the Code of Conduct every term of council.

01:58:03.390 --> 01:58:07.670
So back in 2023, we started this work and

01:58:08.170 --> 01:58:12.190
unbeknownst to us, the province was also working on some legislation. So we

01:58:12.690 --> 01:58:16.990
worked with John Mascaran, we consulted municipal Best practices

01:58:17.630 --> 01:58:21.600
to develop a revised code of Conduct and the

01:58:22.100 --> 01:58:25.080
policy. And we asked Mr.

01:58:25.580 --> 01:58:29.080
Mascaran to come in in October to provide an update to council, at which point

01:58:29.580 --> 01:58:33.720
we were ready to come with our revised code. And then about six

01:58:34.220 --> 01:58:37.880
weeks later, the province introduced the Municipal Accountability Act.

01:58:39.000 --> 01:58:42.880
So we held off at that point on doing any

01:58:43.380 --> 01:58:46.960
more work to see what would happen with the legislation and

01:58:47.460 --> 01:58:51.390
with the, the provincial election. And just a

01:58:51.470 --> 01:58:55.230
week ago, two weeks ago, the province has reintroduced the act.

01:58:55.630 --> 01:58:58.910
So I'll give you a quick update of what's in that.

01:58:59.870 --> 01:59:03.590
The most significant thing for us with

01:59:04.090 --> 01:59:07.470
respect to the code is that should the act be approved,

01:59:08.430 --> 01:59:12.190
it will give the province the authority to pass regulations

01:59:12.430 --> 01:59:16.560
that will create a standardized code of conduct for all municipalities

01:59:17.060 --> 01:59:21.120
in the province. So it will also create an

01:59:21.620 --> 01:59:25.240
advisory role for the Provincial Integrity Commissioner to

01:59:25.400 --> 01:59:29.800
give training to all municipal Integrity commissioners and councillors

01:59:30.300 --> 01:59:33.880
on the Code of Conduct. And then the other big

01:59:34.380 --> 01:59:38.760
piece of it is that it will provide a mechanism whereby councillors

01:59:38.840 --> 01:59:42.120
can be removed from council should there be

01:59:42.620 --> 01:59:47.000
a significant violation of the code. And then

01:59:47.500 --> 01:59:52.040
there would be an investigation from the Municipal Integrity Commissioner, a recommendation

01:59:52.120 --> 01:59:55.520
from the Provincial Integrity Commissioner, and then a unanimous vote

01:59:56.020 --> 01:59:59.640
from the rest of council to remove somebody. So it's a pretty high bar,

02:00:00.120 --> 02:00:03.480
but that will be what happens should this act pass.

02:00:04.280 --> 02:00:08.040
So we talked with Mr. Mascaran about what

02:00:08.540 --> 02:00:11.760
we thought we should do with this, and the province has,

02:00:12.000 --> 02:00:14.880
because it would make our code of conduct obsolete. So we,

02:00:14.960 --> 02:00:17.760
we said, is this something we want to bring forward?

02:00:18.240 --> 02:00:22.080
And in essence we're still recommending that council goes forward with the new code

02:00:22.480 --> 02:00:26.080
because the province has said that if the act passes,

02:00:26.320 --> 02:00:30.440
the standardized code won't be in place until after the

02:00:30.940 --> 02:00:35.740
next municipal election. So we still have 18 months where the

02:00:36.240 --> 02:00:39.780
option is an out of date code or the new revised code, which Chris

02:00:40.280 --> 02:00:44.140
is going to walk you through the significant changes. The other big

02:00:44.640 --> 02:00:48.060
reason that we're recommending going forward with it is the Municipal Accountability

02:00:48.560 --> 02:00:52.660
act doesn't touch the requirement for

02:00:53.460 --> 02:00:55.940
municipalities to have a council staff relations policy.

02:00:56.660 --> 02:01:00.550
And our current council staff relations policy highly

02:01:01.050 --> 02:01:04.550
references the Code of conduct. So the new

02:01:05.050 --> 02:01:08.710
one doesn't. It's a better, more standalone

02:01:09.210 --> 02:01:12.790
document. So if council doesn't wish to

02:01:13.290 --> 02:01:16.750
Go forward with the recommendation tonight. That's fine. We will probably

02:01:17.250 --> 02:01:20.990
be back after the Municipal Accountability act passes with the

02:01:21.490 --> 02:01:24.990
new council staff relations policy because we would need to revise that anyway.

02:01:25.720 --> 02:01:29.360
So with that context, Chris has done all the heavy lifting

02:01:29.860 --> 02:01:33.480
and will answer all the tough questions about all the proposed changes to the document.

02:01:33.640 --> 02:01:36.760
He can probably work the buttons better too. Thank you, Tim.

02:01:36.920 --> 02:01:40.760
Regional Chair Members of Council for now, I'll be going over what the major

02:01:41.260 --> 02:01:44.640
changes that we noticed are for the code of conduct that'll

02:01:45.140 --> 02:01:48.600
apply to you guys. First and foremost, the applicability to advisory

02:01:49.100 --> 02:01:52.930
committees. So as it stands right now, there's separate documents for each. What we're proposing

02:01:53.430 --> 02:01:56.770
is universally applicable to both. The gift section has been updated

02:01:57.270 --> 02:02:00.730
to reflect best practices and the introduction of a new clause known

02:02:01.230 --> 02:02:05.130
as non pecuniary interest, which is essentially the ability to declare conflict on

02:02:05.630 --> 02:02:08.930
items that are not strictly financial. There are also a number of administrative

02:02:09.430 --> 02:02:13.210
changes that we're proposing. Change to gender neutral language that should be

02:02:13.710 --> 02:02:17.090
fairly self evident. We remove the different treatment for tickets to events

02:02:17.590 --> 02:02:20.870
as that's now neatly encapsulated by the general provisions of

02:02:21.370 --> 02:02:24.990
the gift section. And then the Council staff relations policy which

02:02:25.490 --> 02:02:29.310
expands on the roles of council and staff that are already existing and working with

02:02:29.810 --> 02:02:32.990
the direction from the CAO as it pertains

02:02:33.490 --> 02:02:38.110
to advisory committee and local boards. As I mentioned, as it stands the current structure

02:02:38.610 --> 02:02:42.230
is a different code for each or proposing is a universally applicable one for both,

02:02:42.310 --> 02:02:45.770
especially since the one that governs advisory committees

02:02:46.270 --> 02:02:50.410
is a little underdeveloped and this would also allow for a standardized complaint process through

02:02:50.910 --> 02:02:55.090
the appropriate means of the Integrity Commissioner. There is also still the retained

02:02:55.590 --> 02:02:58.850
ability for council to apply penalties for advisory committees

02:02:59.350 --> 02:03:02.850
or local boards when the Integrity Commissioner does not, and also vice

02:03:03.350 --> 02:03:06.970
versa for advisory committees to be able to implement penalties where

02:03:07.470 --> 02:03:09.360
Integrity Commissioner does not recommend a sanction.

02:03:11.110 --> 02:03:14.950
The gift section has also been updated the previous code.

02:03:15.450 --> 02:03:18.870
Again, a lot of the content is similar, but there are some now general provisions

02:03:19.370 --> 02:03:22.710
which still maintain that prohibition against undue influence.

02:03:22.790 --> 02:03:26.270
And there is also some more permissive aspects. So some of

02:03:26.770 --> 02:03:31.070
the more notable ones are the volunteerism that we didn't want to unreasonably

02:03:31.570 --> 02:03:34.550
limit the items that are subject to randomness.

02:03:35.050 --> 02:03:38.680
So lottery tickets, those kind of things that you might receive

02:03:39.180 --> 02:03:42.480
at events and that kind of thing, and also items that are already available for

02:03:42.980 --> 02:03:46.240
public use. So the reasoning being that if they're already publicly available

02:03:46.740 --> 02:03:51.040
there isn't that element of undue influence. There is also now a simplified process

02:03:51.200 --> 02:03:54.760
in consultation with the Integrity Commissioner, the provision of

02:03:55.260 --> 02:03:59.280
the gift registry was deemed unnecessary and so we decided to focus instead on

02:03:59.680 --> 02:04:03.480
gifts that you can and can't receive. And that's, that's neatly reflected in

02:04:03.980 --> 02:04:07.440
the new provisions for gift reception. Being conscious

02:04:07.940 --> 02:04:10.840
as well of the lobbyist registry that was just adopted, we did do have a

02:04:11.340 --> 02:04:14.680
little note there that just emphasizes coherence to the

02:04:15.180 --> 02:04:18.240
provisions in the lobbyist registry and that associated code of conduct, just so there's no

02:04:18.740 --> 02:04:22.480
blurred lines there. And as I already mentioned, there's a new administrative change about

02:04:22.980 --> 02:04:26.960
removing that separate treatment for tickets to events, as that's already captured

02:04:27.460 --> 02:04:29.230
by the provisions of the, the gift section.

02:04:30.990 --> 02:04:34.350
Non pecuniary interests. So, as you may know and recall from

02:04:34.850 --> 02:04:39.350
John's presentation, the Municipal Conflict of Interest act primarily deals with pecuniary

02:04:39.850 --> 02:04:43.670
interests or financial conflicts. So what this provision seeks

02:04:44.170 --> 02:04:47.830
to address is situations where counsel might believe they have a non

02:04:48.330 --> 02:04:51.750
financial conflict and that they wish to use this tool to make

02:04:52.250 --> 02:04:55.750
that declaration. And so there are situations you could potentially

02:04:56.250 --> 02:04:59.590
imagine, you know, appointments or hires and that kind of thing, where it's maybe

02:05:00.090 --> 02:05:03.990
not entirely clear that it's a financial interest. But as a counselor we've seen you

02:05:04.490 --> 02:05:07.310
maybe want to be able to declare a conflict anyway and this will then allow

02:05:07.810 --> 02:05:11.390
you to do that. We included a deemed absent provision associated with this

02:05:11.890 --> 02:05:15.750
to address a potential loophole that comes in the Municipal act if you're absent

02:05:16.250 --> 02:05:18.830
for a vote, being a negative vote. We figure that if you didn't want something

02:05:19.330 --> 02:05:22.350
to pass through the conflict, that that should still be addressed anyways. And so that

02:05:22.850 --> 02:05:26.270
loophole doesn't exist. So that just seeks to clean that up a little more

02:05:26.770 --> 02:05:30.510
neatly. And lastly, as mentioned, we're proposing a new

02:05:31.010 --> 02:05:35.270
